#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320950 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98c0bcef-46b2-545e-843c-328afdee9423" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319845}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319845} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4fb75ca-ef4f-5f30-adaf-5e38a644e069" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320493}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6f433502-a7f8-5681-8b4b-ad43723d3a55" :question/math-academy-id "q-352075" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation ${f}^{′}(x)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0$ that satisfies the condition $f(0)=2.$\n\n$f(x)=\\,${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ f(x) & =-3\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+C \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our general solution is $f(x)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C.$ To find a particular solution, we substitute the condition $f(0)=2$ into our solution and obtain a value for $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+C \\\\ 2 & =3(1)+C \\\\ C & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\nf(x)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321077 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6216e04b-6d56-5762-a78b-734a7a46769c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "612" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319687}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319687} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9e54fb6-a7eb-5b9e-ab0a-fcb7b790d76e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320592}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "bc7e694f-b4b0-55d0-bb20-74aed5966295" :question/math-academy-id "q-353180" :question/problem "The amount of a rare plant species $A(t)$ after $t$ years can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=0.072A.\n$$\n\nIf there are currently $320$ plants, calculate the amount after $9$ years. Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\nThe amount of the plant species after $9$ years is approximately \n{{field-1}}\nplants." :question/worked-solution "We model the amount using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=0.072A.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nA(t)={A}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.072t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${A}_{0}$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that there are currently $320$ plants, so we have ${A}_{0}=320.$\n\nTherefore, the amount $A$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nA(t)=320\\,{e}^{0.072t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the amount when $t=9,$ we substitute $t=9$ into the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(9) & =320\\,{e}^{0.072\\cdot 9} \\\\  & =320\\,{e}^{0.648} \\\\  & ≈611.53\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding our answer to the nearest integer, we conclude that the amount of the plant species after $9$ years is approximately $612$ plants."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321079 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3829bbff-9be4-569c-ace1-36fb7968ae99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4803" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319564}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319564} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6fd2b30-8369-5e17-93bd-35f33bcd455d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320641}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "dd183ed4-aa74-5e82-bf56-bd34f83720dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-353186" :question/problem "The number of cells in a petri dish $C(t)$ after $t$ hours can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=C.\n$$\n\nIf there are currently $650$ cells in the petri dish, calculate the number of cells after $2$ hours. Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\nThe number of cells after $2$ hours is approximately \n{{field-1}}\ncells." :question/worked-solution "We model the number of cells using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=C.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nC(t)={C}_{0}\\,{e}^{t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{0}$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that there are currently $650$ cells in the petri dish, so we have ${C}_{0}=650.$\n\nTherefore, the number of cells $C$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nC(t)=650\\,{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the number of cells when $t=2,$ we substitute $t=2$ into the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(2) & =650\\,{e}^{2} \\\\  & ≈4802.89\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding our answer to the nearest integer, we conclude that the number of cells after $2$ hours is approximately $4803$ cells."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321080 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d9575de7-2bf5-5ca5-bce4-7fa34f8fddee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6552" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320052}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320052} :answer-field/id #uuid "62fe0b92-74c5-5721-a415-075709bd333a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320372}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8f062774-f5b6-5715-9510-abfa22379f04" :question/math-academy-id "q-353189" :question/problem "The number of insects in a controlled habitat $H(t)$ after $t$ months can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=2H.\n$$\n\nIf there are currently $120$ insects in the habitat, calculate the number of insects after $2$ months. Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\nThe number of insects after $2$ months is approximately \n{{field-1}}\ninsects." :question/worked-solution "We model the number of insects using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=2H.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nH(t)={H}_{0}\\,{e}^{2t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${H}_{0}$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that there are currently $120$ insects in the habitat, so we have ${H}_{0}=120.$\n\nTherefore, the number of insects $H$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nH(t)=120\\,{e}^{2t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the number of insects when $t=2,$ we substitute $t=2$ into the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}H(2) & =120\\,{e}^{2\\cdot 2} \\\\  & =120\\,{e}^{4} \\\\  & ≈6551.78\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding our answer to the nearest integer, we conclude that the number of insects after $2$ months is approximately $6552$ insects."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321082 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1422b8ae-ccba-51e8-b113-3dd7c120782c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319447}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319447} :answer-field/id #uuid "681ce303-01b9-5dab-ba35-1774b8f64920" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320379}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2bd3368e-0e3f-5373-9613-b9232c8c8ce1" :question/math-academy-id "q-353197" :question/problem "The population of a bacterial culture $P(t)$ after $t$ hours can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=-P.\n$$\n\nIf there are currently $30$ bacteria in the culture, calculate the population after $3$ hours. Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\nThe population after $3$ hours is approximately \n{{field-1}}\nbacteria." :question/worked-solution "We model the population using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=-P.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)={P}_{0}\\,{e}^{-t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{0}$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that there are currently $30$ bacteria in the culture, so we have ${P}_{0}=30.$\n\nTherefore, the population $P$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nP(t)=30\\,{e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the population when $t=3,$ we substitute $t=3$ into the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3) & =30\\,{e}^{-1\\cdot 3} \\\\  & =30\\,{e}^{-3} \\\\  & ≈1.49\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding our answer to the nearest integer, we conclude that the population after $3$ hours is approximately $1$ bacterium."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321083 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a4d6edd-79c2-5c74-8dc6-504578e0856a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319855}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319855} :answer-field/id #uuid "8912c0bd-736a-5e2d-a592-ca76e1eaee47" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320445}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5ffc0670-7032-5618-9938-5d5033bcb5b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-353210" :question/problem "The number of bacteria $N=N(t)$ in a laboratory culture $t$ hours after observation can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=kN\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is the growth rate. If there were $500$ bacteria initially, and $N(3)=900$, calculate the value of the growth rate $k$ to the nearest percent.\n\n$k≈$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\%$" :question/worked-solution "We model the number of bacteria using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=kN.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nN(t)={N}_{0}\\,{e}^{kt},\n$$\n\nwhere ${N}_{0}$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that there were $500$ bacteria initially, so we have ${N}_{0}=500.$\n\nTherefore, the number of bacteria $N$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nN(t)=500\\,{e}^{kt}.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of $k,$ we substitute $N(3)=900$ into the equation above and solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(3) & =500\\,{e}^{k\\cdot 3} \\\\ 900 & =500\\,{e}^{3k} \\\\ {e}^{3k} & =\\frac{900}{500} \\\\ {e}^{3k} & =\\frac{9}{5} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{3k}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{9}{5}\\right) \\\\ 3k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{9}{5}\\right) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{9}{5}\\right) \\\\ k & ≈0.1959\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k≈20\\%,$ rounded to the nearest percent."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321084 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5308342e-0079-53b8-96c0-a1226d25ca27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319631}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319631} :answer-field/id #uuid "0fff7544-68af-54e4-a42e-daa502e7690e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320194}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3f112590-ba59-50ed-81c5-89311d726307" :question/math-academy-id "q-353211" :question/problem "The number of subscribers $S=S(t)$ to a website $t$ months after a marketing campaign can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=kS\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is the growth rate. If there were $12\\,000$ subscribers initially, and $S(2)=15\\,000$, calculate the value of the growth rate $k$ to the nearest percent.\n\n$k≈$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\%$" :question/worked-solution "We model the number of subscribers using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=kS.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nS(t)={S}_{0}\\,{e}^{kt},\n$$\n\nwhere ${S}_{0}$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that there were $12\\,000$ subscribers initially, so we have ${S}_{0}=12\\,000.$\n\nTherefore, the number of subscribers $S$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nS(t)=12\\,000\\,{e}^{kt}.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of $k,$ we substitute $S(2)=15\\,000$ into the equation above and solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(2) & =12\\,000\\,{e}^{k\\cdot 2} \\\\ 15\\,000 & =12\\,000\\,{e}^{2k} \\\\ {e}^{2k} & =\\frac{15\\,000}{12\\,000} \\\\ {e}^{2k} & =\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2k}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right) \\\\ 2k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right) \\\\ k & ≈0.111\\,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k≈11\\%,$ rounded to the nearest percent."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323684 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8afe1cc-6a73-59a7-a3aa-86abc400ccd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{cos}(4x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322470}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322470} :answer-field/id #uuid "549d1fa9-dbbb-5a28-9ec0-95d21eef029c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323329}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6a3b691d-ce23-5509-8fc2-d490e3df8940" :question/math-academy-id "q-355525" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3y){y}^{′}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)=0$ that satisfies $y\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)=0.$ Assume that $-\\frac{\\pi }{6}<y<\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3y)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3y)\\,\\text{d}y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3y)\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3y) & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+{C}_{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3y) & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=3{C}_{1}.$\n\nWe now apply the condition $y\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)=0$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot 0) & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8})+C \\\\ 0 & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+C \\\\ 0 & =0+C \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the particular solution in implicit form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3y) & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323685 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a897ef12-2c09-5096-a8b5-ee77680d8d9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "({x}^{2}+2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322468}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322468} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea786b33-77be-5d8b-adac-7883d2cd2257" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323631}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e4bca2e9-3423-5b05-8658-606cf78e9ed4" :question/math-academy-id "q-355528" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4x\\sqrt{y}$\nthat satisfies the condition $y(1)=9.$  Assume that $y>0.$\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find the particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4x\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\text{d}y & =4x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int 4x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 2\\sqrt{y} & =2{x}^{2}+{C}_{1} \\\\ \\sqrt{y} & ={x}^{2}+\\frac{{C}_{1}}{2} \\\\ y & ={({x}^{2}+\\frac{{C}_{1}}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssuming $y>0,$ the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)=({x}^{2}+C)^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $C=\\frac{{C}_{1}}{2}$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(1)=9$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9 & =((1)^{2}+C)^{2} \\\\ 9 & =(1+C)^{2} \\\\ 3 & =1+C \\\\ C & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)=({x}^{2}+2)^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323686 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96ae4175-e85f-5fa0-9c66-e85a778d8d55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{7}\\operatorname{tan}(7x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322350}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322350} :answer-field/id #uuid "aef3a27e-eb31-5e68-9379-c97a63bec369" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323508}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "64377a61-5efe-5da5-9c93-affa5fd1266f" :question/math-academy-id "q-355544" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation ${sec}^{2}⁡(4y){y}^{′}={sec}^{2}⁡(7x)$ that satisfies $y(0)=0.$ Assume that $-\\frac{\\pi }{8}<y<\\frac{\\pi }{8}.$\n\n$\\operatorname{tan}⁡(4y)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sec}^{2}⁡(4y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={sec}^{2}⁡(7x) \\\\ {sec}^{2}⁡(4y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={sec}^{2}⁡(7x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {sec}^{2}⁡(4y)\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡(7x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(4y) & =\\frac{1}{7}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(7x)+{C}_{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡(4y) & =\\frac{4}{7}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(7x)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=4{C}_{1}.$\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(0)=0$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡(4\\cdot 0) & =\\frac{4}{7}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(7\\cdot 0)+C \\\\ 0 & =0+C \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the particular solution in implicit form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(4y) & =\\frac{4}{7}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(7x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323687 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f64a635e-2029-5d66-8938-817f402fbc99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{x}+4{x}^{2}+7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323077}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323077} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d3128ea-a07c-5f2f-ad3f-00a6446136d1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323290}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6e98fc5d-ad7f-574e-b282-efe5e38c1e1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-355546" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation ${x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-8{x}^{3}=5$ that satisfies $y(1)=6.$\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-8{x}^{3} & =5 \\\\ {x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =5+8{x}^{3} \\\\ \\text{d}y & =(5{x}^{-2}+8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\text{d}y & =\\int (5{x}^{-2}+8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =-5{x}^{-1}+4{x}^{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now apply the initial condition $y(1)=6$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =6 \\\\ -5(1)^{-1}+4(1)^{2}+C & =6 \\\\ C-1 & =6 \\\\ C & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =-5{x}^{-1}+4{x}^{2}+7 \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{x}+4{x}^{2}+7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323688 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13eaf0de-4e0e-5ea8-907d-4d9968125b18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{x}+2{x}^{2}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321308}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321308} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc4ee59c-22f5-5a40-b03d-278d405cc912" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323609}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "75023361-eb11-537e-8a6b-dbd0dee1ad40" :question/math-academy-id "q-355547" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\sqrt{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-4x\\sqrt{x}=3$ that satisfies $y(1)=11.$\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-4x\\sqrt{x} & =3 \\\\ \\sqrt{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3+4x\\sqrt{x} \\\\ \\text{d}y & =(3{x}^{-1/2}+4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\text{d}y & =\\int (3{x}^{-1/2}+4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =6{x}^{1/2}+2{x}^{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now apply the initial condition $y(1)=11$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =11 \\\\ 6(1)^{1/2}+2(1)^{2}+C & =11 \\\\ C+8 & =11 \\\\ C & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =6{x}^{1/2}+2{x}^{2}+3 \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{x}+2{x}^{2}+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323689 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2055e3dd-2e6d-5b82-b38d-ca3921647471" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2{e}^{2{x}^{2}}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321390}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321390} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f37f1d2-7d30-5928-8c23-707d4ffb2cc7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323197}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "af7393d2-efb0-5146-b448-33e99ed4412d" :question/math-academy-id "q-355559" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-4x{y}^{2}=4x$ that satisfies $y(0)=1.$ Assume that $y>0.$\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-4x{y}^{2} & =4x \\\\ 2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4x{y}^{2}+4x \\\\ 2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4x({y}^{2}+1) \\\\ \\frac{2y}{{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}y & =4x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{2y}{{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int 4x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1) & =2{x}^{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExponentiating both sides, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1)} & ={e}^{2{x}^{2}+C} \\\\ {y}^{2}+1 & =C{e}^{2{x}^{2}} \\\\ y & =\\sqrt{C{e}^{2{x}^{2}}-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we used that $y>0.$\n\nWe now apply the initial condition $y(0)=1$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =1 \\\\ \\sqrt{C{e}^{2(0)^{2}}-1} & =1 \\\\ \\sqrt{C-1} & =1 \\\\ C-1 & =1 \\\\ C & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)=\\sqrt{2{e}^{2{x}^{2}}-1}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323690 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2e265e1-c3cb-5fab-b4f5-ccf995516d4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{{x}^{2}/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322433}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322433} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b3e560d-f350-5b7b-bd75-6283cbcfd045" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323221}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bfb5c5d2-d9d1-5d15-b3d0-8a3479b9922a" :question/math-academy-id "q-355562" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=xy$  that satisfies $y(0)=1.$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find the particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =xy \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\,x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+C\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the exponential function to both sides, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|} & ={e}^{{x}^{2}/2+C} \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{{x}^{2}/2+C}\\, \\\\ y & =\\pm {e}^{{x}^{2}/2+C} \\\\ y & =K{e}^{{x}^{2}/2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is an arbitrary constant. \n\nWe now apply the condition $y(0)=1$ to find $K:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =K{e}^{0} \\\\ K & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=xy$ that satisfies the condition $y(0)=1$ is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{{x}^{2}/2}\\,.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323691 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11d2d065-70d1-5174-a4e1-728f26ee993f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2x-1){e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321286}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321286} :answer-field/id #uuid "a70e7a74-f88a-5b4f-8fac-b4f7d571a35d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323490}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "aabfb888-4d5b-5ee3-a31d-caf8a930a357" :question/math-academy-id "q-355564" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $$\n\\displaystyle (-\\frac{1}{xy}+\\frac{2}{y})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4\n$$ that satisfies $y(1)={e}^{2}.$\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-\\frac{1}{xy}+\\frac{2}{y})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{2x-1}{xy}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{4x}{2x-1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2+\\frac{2}{2x-1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =(2+\\frac{2}{2x-1})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int (2+\\frac{2}{2x-1})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =2x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the exponential function to both sides, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|} & ={e}^{2x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|+C} \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{2x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|+C} \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{2x}\\cdot {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|}\\cdot {e}^{C} \\\\ y & =K(2x-1){e}^{2x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is an arbitrary constant. \n\nWe now apply the initial condition $y(1)={e}^{2}$ to find $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & ={e}^{2} \\\\ K(2(1)-1){e}^{2(1)} & ={e}^{2} \\\\ K{e}^{2} & ={e}^{2} \\\\ K & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)=(2x-1){e}^{2x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323692 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90c5bd0e-992a-5060-a1b1-7adb29cef3ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{-{e}^{-2x}+2{e}^{2}+1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322298}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322298} :answer-field/id #uuid "15f6b0da-0d33-5c51-ac9b-a4198b00fb73" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323209}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "805542c0-bca8-5f60-bf27-701c39eb6996" :question/math-academy-id "q-355576" :question/problem "Find the specific solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{-2x-y}$ given that  $\\,y(0)=2.$\n\n$y(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-1}} $)$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{-2x-y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{-2x}{e}^{-y} \\\\ {e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{-2x} \\\\ \\int {e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int {e}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {e}^{y} & =-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-2x}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the natural logarithm to both sides, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{y}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-2x}+C) \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-2x}+C).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(0)=2$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =2 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{0}+C) & =2 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(C-\\frac{1}{2}) & =2 \\\\ C-\\frac{1}{2} & ={e}^{2} \\\\ C & =\\frac{2{e}^{2}+1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the particular solution of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{-2x-y}$ that satisfies the condition $y(0)=2$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{-{e}^{-2x}+2{e}^{2}+1}{2}\\right).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323693 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7cecbd4b-7276-5711-93be-756561f37cee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}(\\frac{5}{4}-{e}^{-x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322129}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322129} :answer-field/id #uuid "19779a17-730f-59fa-bfa1-c2bc8e54d880" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323218}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a6ea94de-6671-5cd2-9382-54cb9545def9" :question/math-academy-id "q-355577" :question/problem "Find the specific solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{-x-y}$ given that $\\,y(0)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4.$\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{-x-y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{-x}{e}^{-y} \\\\ {e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{-x} \\\\ \\int {e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {e}^{y} & =-{e}^{-x}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the natural logarithm to both sides, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{y}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-{e}^{-x}+C) \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-{e}^{-x}+C).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(0)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(-{e}^{0}+C) & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(C-1) & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 \\\\ C-1 & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ C & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the particular solution of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{-x-y}$ that satisfies the condition $y(0)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{4}-{e}^{-x}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323694 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec80c9e4-ade4-588f-871a-59a453ae5b9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2-1/x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323008}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323008} :answer-field/id #uuid "afe21afb-2492-52bc-ac68-6f00ab45aa76" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323510}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "debef920-e2c6-5181-8631-87e6b6d975ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-355578" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}=0\n$$  that satisfies $y(1)=e.$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find the particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\,\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =-\\frac{1}{x}+C\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the exponential function to both sides, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|} & ={e}^{-1/x+C} \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{-1/x+C}\\, \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{-1/x}\\cdot {e}^{C} \\\\ y & =K{e}^{-1/x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is an arbitrary constant. \n\nWe now apply the condition $y(1)=e$ to find $K:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e & =K{e}^{-1} \\\\ K & ={e}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution of $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}=0\n$$ that satisfies the condition $y(1)=e$ is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{2-1/x}\\,.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323695 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1346830d-69d5-5e73-b008-864e705c06e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2\\sqrt{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321295}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321295} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4536973-5a71-5025-9fa7-f6117f951929" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323487}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "79c0a17d-464c-5b60-a5f6-f47a7f26752e" :question/math-academy-id "q-355579" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{x}}\n$$  that satisfies $y(1)={e}^{2}.$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find the particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & =2\\sqrt{x}+C\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the exponential function to both sides, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|} & ={e}^{2\\sqrt{x}+C} \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{2\\sqrt{x}+C}\\, \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{2\\sqrt{x}}\\cdot {e}^{C} \\\\ y & =K{e}^{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is an arbitrary constant. \n\nWe now apply the condition $y(1)={e}^{2}$ to find $K:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2} & =K{e}^{2\\sqrt{1}} \\\\ {e}^{2} & =K{e}^{2} \\\\ K & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution of $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{x}}\n$$ that satisfies the condition $y(1)={e}^{2}$ is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323696 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d94f85d-4906-5228-9ed0-d96a48b11c86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}(2-{e}^{-x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321501}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321501} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e8e425b-6779-5e93-9532-18cad7118f4f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323392}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b704df68-ffc0-57d3-acee-bb78600fdc0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-355582" :question/problem "Find the specific solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{-x-y}$ given that  $\\,y(0)=0.$\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{-x-y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{-x}{e}^{-y} \\\\ {e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{-x} \\\\ \\int {e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int {e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {e}^{y} & =-{e}^{-x}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the natural logarithm to both sides, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{y}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-{e}^{-x}+C) \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-{e}^{-x}+C).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(0)=0$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(-{e}^{0}+C) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(C-1) & =0 \\\\ C-1 & =1 \\\\ C & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the particular solution of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{-x-y}$ that satisfies the condition $y(0)=0$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-{e}^{-x}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323888 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ac56bb3-0094-5d6a-8531-71dbd69471d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=5\\,700-0.8P,\\,P(0)=7\\,200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321476} {:answer/id #uuid "a788a3f8-9530-54ec-9aea-383d1c4e9378" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.02P-875,\\,P(0)=47\\,500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322459} {:answer/id #uuid "a7a3f83e-f687-58bc-bf83-3f81da84826d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=22P-19\\,734,\\,P(0)=20\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322460} {:answer/id #uuid "ded2b71b-d572-5a02-a041-f687bff48436" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=690-0.003P,\\,P(0)=200\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322889} {:answer/id #uuid "efa861a6-0396-5d58-8e08-d970115f8b51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=1\\,250-0.005P,\\,P(0)=270\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323036}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322889} :answer-field/id #uuid "6bac56d3-4bd4-55bb-aa6f-dc8f9891aec6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323386}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "09b550d2-2e2e-5317-b1f2-d1a7002c555c" :question/math-academy-id "q-37176" :question/problem "Which of the following initial value problems is an inhibited growth problem?" :question/worked-solution "An inhibited growth initial value problem has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=k(a-y),\\,y(0)={y}_{0}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited growth problem is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=690-0.003P,\\,P(0)=200\\,000,\n$$\n\nsince\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=690-0.003P=0.003(230\\,000-P).\n$$\n\nIn this case, $k=0.003,a=230\\,000,$ and ${y}_{0}=200\\,000.$ Notice that $a>{y}_{0},$ which is required for inhibited growth. \n\nThe other options do not represent inhibited growth. In particular, the initial value problems\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=1\\,250-0.005P=0.005(250\\,000-P),\\,P(0)=270\\,000,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=5\\,700-0.8P=0.8(7\\,125-P),\\,P(0)=7\\,200\n$$\n\ndo *not* represent an inhibited growth model because ${y}_{0}=270\\,000>a=250\\,000,$ and ${y}_{0}=7\\,200>a=7\\,125.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323889 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07308918-08ed-546a-b64f-46dfcd23d16c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=7-0.000\\,2P,\\,P(0)=38\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321204} {:answer/id #uuid "13709d88-4880-5079-9d80-c2aaa3ad82de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=8\\,200-40P,\\,P(0)=210" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321300} {:answer/id #uuid "188baeac-33cc-5322-843c-5f6944933b5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=3P-321,\\,P(0)=120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321342} {:answer/id #uuid "ad2aba9e-fe52-5134-9cf6-3a51499c74bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=450-0.03P,\\,P(0)=16\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322505} {:answer/id #uuid "f61d44bc-6d22-5aa1-bb6f-c94245c1e038" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=830-0.8P,\\,P(0)=1\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323076}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323076} :answer-field/id #uuid "29089e7f-1a1a-5390-8184-20fce54bffdd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323255}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b1526a51-a5e8-57d9-af02-90947e9133d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-37177" :question/problem "Which of the following initial value problems is an inhibited growth problem?" :question/worked-solution "An inhibited growth initial value problem has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=k(a-y),\\,y(0)={y}_{0}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited growth problem is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=830-0.8P,\\,P(0)=1\\,000,\n$$\n\nsince\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=830-0.8P=0.8(1\\,037.5-P).\n$$\n\nIn this case, $k=0.8,a=1\\,037.5,$ and ${y}_{0}=1\\,000.$ Notice that $a>{y}_{0},$ which is required for inhibited growth. \n\nThe other options do not represent inhibited growth. In particular, the initial value problems\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=7-0.000\\,2P=0.000\\,2(35\\,000-P),\\,P(0)=38\\,000,\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=450-0.03P=0.03(15\\,000-P),\\,P(0)=16\\,000,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=8\\,200-40P=40(205-P),\\,P(0)=210\n$$\n\ndo *not* represent an inhibited growth model because ${y}_{0}=38\\,000>a=35\\,000,$ ${y}_{0}=16\\,000>a=15\\,000,$ and ${y}_{0}=210>a=205.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326685 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "640f91af-9af3-58fc-8a63-9b8699619aae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324801} {:answer/id #uuid "a2b6266a-0178-58c9-afc0-b11ab26e4082" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325351} {:answer/id #uuid "d6bbf60c-21a8-5de6-bc41-6cabb8ede0a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325839} {:answer/id #uuid "ede03381-3d73-5674-a3cc-1b91d88fe642" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326063} {:answer/id #uuid "ef576b5f-b070-572f-99c9-6414e6f1049a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326082}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325839} :answer-field/id #uuid "40ee238c-35a5-5dad-bb56-ba165c7577fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326326}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "12b9ca6b-0ddb-5ee4-aeb2-e1fcfdc8bcb6" :question/math-academy-id "q-3850" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=x{e}^{-y}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x{e}^{-y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{x}{{e}^{y}} \\\\ {e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\cancel{\\text{d}x}}\\,\\cancel{\\text{d}x} & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {e}^{y} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n{e}^{y}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+C.\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y=y(x).$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+C).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326690 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36403495-4819-509a-932f-4c97f8aa7b1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={e}^{{x}^{2}}+{e}^{{x}^{3}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324413} {:answer/id #uuid "4fd5ef59-50c9-5c92-95c9-4ed4edd2d608" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=K{e}^{{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324625} {:answer/id #uuid "5fa29fba-6a67-5365-afb0-3f19aca8c7ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y={x}^{2}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324770} {:answer/id #uuid "c652480f-f0c2-5459-9c4e-e7870338385a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325684} {:answer/id #uuid "d4dff042-5ffc-5451-8cbe-3ca7735819e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{3}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325823}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324625} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d052acb-260c-5495-945e-851283aac703" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326446}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f1ee3e58-1671-5aa8-8cb2-98c85793fdad" :question/math-academy-id "q-3854" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation ${y}^{′}=2yx+y{x}^{2}$ for $y\\ne 0.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2yx+y{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2yx+y{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y(2x+{x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x+{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2x+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int (2x+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & ={x}^{2}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|={x}^{2}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+C.\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y=y(x).$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y| & ={x}^{2}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+C \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}/3+C} \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}/3}\\cdot {e}^{C} \\\\ |y| & =K{e}^{{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}/3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K={e}^{C}$ and $K>0.$\n\nWe can drop the absolute value bars by allowing $K$ to be either positive or negative. Therefore, the most general solution is\n\n$$\ny=K{e}^{{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}/3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326809 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "502004df-a0be-5cea-878e-09803c5e34d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{e}^{x}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324628} {:answer/id #uuid "5babf9ef-2007-5ac3-b1a7-d3a7000142a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-{e}^{x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324728} {:answer/id #uuid "9f32f367-c166-57dd-a1ca-a5e258aafc79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325321} {:answer/id #uuid "cb26728e-8a5c-5d88-940f-50ca2ae95d4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{{e}^{x}+1}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325734} {:answer/id #uuid "f04334c1-fe98-564e-8bda-076b98d63f11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4{e}^{x}-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326091}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324628} :answer-field/id #uuid "2fa3b410-49d2-532c-b067-882d1961f404" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326303}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "45459917-ee60-5543-8543-ded7c1f55344" :question/math-academy-id "q-4016" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x-2y}$ that satisfies $y(0)=0.$" :question/worked-solution "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{x-2y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{-2y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2y}} \\\\ {e}^{2y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{x} \\\\ \\int {e}^{2y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {e}^{2y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2y} & ={e}^{x}+{C}_{1} \\\\ {e}^{2y} & =2{e}^{x}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=2{C}_{1}$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nApplying the natural logarithm to both sides, we find the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2y}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{e}^{x}+C) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{e}^{x}+C).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(0)=0$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{e}^{0}+C) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+C) & =0 \\\\ 2+C & =1 \\\\ C & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the particular solution of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x-2y}$ that satisfies the condition $y(0)=0$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{e}^{x}-1).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329850 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "016b59fa-7dd1-5430-8a0d-9d17b256b87e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8{e}^{4x-1}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326950} {:answer/id #uuid "1a09b33b-87d5-53a9-afee-f8e02962d4c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{4x-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327160} {:answer/id #uuid "35f572ac-b945-560e-89f5-d45ed6858e1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8{e}^{4x-1}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327403} {:answer/id #uuid "bab77687-86e3-5b97-960a-d832566921c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8{e}^{4x-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328563} {:answer/id #uuid "bfa829b5-29ee-5ba4-a1c8-72e5faa0588a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8{e}^{4x-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328611}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328611} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2f4dd66-f6d3-5f50-8ce9-b004c416733f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329462}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "dd0c521a-06b7-559f-8b78-1152ec8cbcd7" :question/math-academy-id "q-48007" :question/problem "Calculate ${f}^{′}(x)$ if $f(x)=-2{e}^{4x-1}+3.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=-2{e}^{u}+3,\\,u=4x-1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=-2{e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-2{e}^{u}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-8{e}^{u} \\\\  & =-8{e}^{4x-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329898 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33b1c38d-bd47-595a-85a4-aaa20457bc59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6{x}^{2}{e}^{8-2{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327383} {:answer/id #uuid "87871190-a65e-5c1e-8282-1faf7bde4a6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(8-6{x}^{2}){e}^{8-2{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328101} {:answer/id #uuid "b8909401-18d1-5019-a6f0-ca794f50ba31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6{x}^{4}{e}^{8-2{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328540} {:answer/id #uuid "c387f8bc-a855-544f-9080-010895d03bd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6{x}^{2}{e}^{8-2{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328643} {:answer/id #uuid "e092d66a-dbb8-5e12-ab82-47da6c4607df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6{x}^{4}{e}^{8-2{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328899}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328643} :answer-field/id #uuid "025f7489-d455-5ba5-a862-9edaba9cd328" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329189}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "97112097-e97d-59a1-a70d-99c7a4daf89b" :question/math-academy-id "q-48629" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ for $f(x)={e}^{8-2{x}^{3}}.$" :question/worked-solution "First we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={e}^{u},\\,u=8-2{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}={e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-6{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot (-6{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-6{x}^{2}{e}^{8-2{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329901 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12a9f96f-82fe-597d-8f9c-ec74365b138f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=20ex\\cdot ln⁡(5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327093} {:answer/id #uuid "341d8922-42cf-54fa-9393-c8b46d49f2db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=5ex" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327386} {:answer/id #uuid "388c97a7-d581-5bec-9e47-92d37546c27d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=ex" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327428} {:answer/id #uuid "569457c0-25f6-5141-b92a-2099597b16ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=4ex" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327685} {:answer/id #uuid "ae9ebfb2-3fe0-510b-bcbc-62f5f335f2b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=20ex" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328449}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328449} :answer-field/id #uuid "40293d79-60e8-50ac-8ded-e02ee37fe2cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329311}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b6722d36-cd26-5e84-89df-067b2a93b540" :question/math-academy-id "q-48657" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y=5{e}^{4x}$ at the point where $x=\\frac{1}{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=5{e}^{u},\\,u=4x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=5{e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =5{e}^{u}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =20{e}^{u} \\\\  & =20{e}^{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the slope of the tangent to the curve at $x=\\frac{1}{4}$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{x=1/4} & ={(20{e}^{4x})|}_{x=1/4} \\\\  & =20{e}^{4(1/4)} \\\\  & =20e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, the $y$-value of the point on the curve where $x={x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & ={(5{e}^{4x})|}_{x=1/4} \\\\  & =5{e}^{4(1/4)} \\\\  & =5e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the equation of the tangent at that point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-5e & =20e(x-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\ y-5e & =20ex-5e \\\\ y & =20ex\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329902 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3844e08c-6f9d-5cf6-a14e-cc030d9a01b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{e}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327425} {:answer/id #uuid "a89f0a93-4737-553c-9bbe-0034bad64d50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328394} {:answer/id #uuid "adef63ee-5bd4-5b53-b409-c9e308d93260" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328445} {:answer/id #uuid "c199d559-8e1d-58ab-8615-2d150595957a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328624} {:answer/id #uuid "d557a2be-477a-515d-91b2-d30108f2cab4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328801}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328445} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0d50368-ac7e-5bb9-86d5-22e01e5b4791" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329554}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fbf43982-c74c-540d-95a5-32f181ac4ea6" :question/math-academy-id "q-48658" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent to the curve $y=-3{e}^{5-x}$ at the point where $x=5-ln⁡(2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=-3{e}^{u},\\,u=5-x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-3{e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-1.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-3{e}^{u}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =3{e}^{u} \\\\  & =3{e}^{5-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the slope of the tangent to the curve at $x=5-ln⁡(2)$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{x=5-ln⁡(2)} & =3{e}^{5-(5-ln⁡(2))} \\\\  & =3{e}^{ln⁡(2)} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329903 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f178a09-59b9-5bd4-94f2-717a9d712eec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327354} {:answer/id #uuid "ce962925-563f-5203-9850-54b8e1527957" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7ln⁡(4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328749} {:answer/id #uuid "d0342368-9d6f-56bf-9428-8ca2563004ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "28ln⁡(4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328765} {:answer/id #uuid "e44279a3-2028-5e38-9e46-7a6b15fa9ce2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328935} {:answer/id #uuid "e877c22b-b0a5-5c6f-80fc-dddf3ca707ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4ln⁡(4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328967}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328765} :answer-field/id #uuid "c745005c-c2dc-5006-b584-ff3b1f2fe9d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329537}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ac2dcbe2-d530-570b-ae79-f45a337889ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-48659" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent to the curve $y={4}^{7x}$ at the point where $x=\\frac{1}{7}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={4}^{u},\\,u=7x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}={4}^{u}ln⁡4,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=7.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={4}^{u}ln⁡4\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =7ln⁡4\\cdot {4}^{u} \\\\  & =7ln⁡4\\cdot {4}^{7x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the slope of the tangent to the curve at $x=\\frac{1}{7}$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{x=1/7} & =7ln⁡(4)\\cdot {4}^{7(1/7)} \\\\  & =7ln⁡(4)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =28ln⁡(4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $x=\\frac{1}{7}$ is $28ln⁡(4).$"}]}
